import sys

from . import life, sub, nim, wythoff

GAMES = {
    'life': life.solve,
    'sub': sub.solve,
    'nim': nim.solve,
    'wythoff': wythoff.solve,
}


def main() -> None:
    game_id = sys.argv[1]
    text = sys.stdin.read()
    sys.stdout.write(GAMES[game_id](text))


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
```

Notes on the two tricky specs:
- `sub`: `WIN m` uses the **numerically smallest** winning first move, so candidates are scanned in sorted step order.
- `wythoff`: moves are only legal if they act on one pile (`i>0, j==0` or `i==0, j>0`) or both equally (`i==j>0`), matching the movement rule; the `(i,j)` scan order gives the lexicographically smallest winning move.

Verified: all 6 public cases match, plus exhaustive brute-force checks for `nim` (all pile configs up to size 15), `sub` (all step sets/n up to 80), `wythoff` (all `1..28`), and 500 random Life cases.
